From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 00:50:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] BD-J host: BD-Live in the service with network, names
 resolved by the player

With BD-Live on, the client connects to the BD-J service with network (/run/hades-bdj/bdj-net.sock,
LIBBLURAY_BDJ_HOST_NET_SOCKET for tests) instead of the one without; where that socket is not
there, the disc runs in the service without network and the log says so.

That service reaches only addresses of the Internet and has no name server: the one of a home
network is on that network or on this system. So the host asks the player (BDR_RESOLVE, protocol
version 2): the player checks that it is given a host name of the Internet and nothing else (no
path, URL, address or local name such as .local, .lan, .home.arpa or a single label), resolves it
with getaddrinfo() on a thread of its own (a slow name server must not hold the reads of the disc
that the host waits for; four at once, twenty a second), and answers only with the IPv4 addresses
BD-Live may reach, against the subnets this system has at that moment. A name that leads only to
this system or its network gets no address at all; that also keeps a name that changes its
address (DNS rebinding) from leading there. Each name is said in the log: resolved, with what was
left out, or refused and why. A late answer after the disc closed is sent to nobody.

In the host, the JVM gets the resolver of the module org.videolan.hades.bdlive (JEP 418, built and
installed by the hades-bdj package), only in the service with network and with BD-Live on:
-Dorg.videolan.hades.bdlive=true, -Djava.net.preferIPv4Stack=true, the module path and
--enable-native-access for that module alone. Its one native method is registered here. The
resolver is chosen at its first use by the class loader of that thread, so Libbluray.init()
uses it before any code of the disc runs: a provider in a disc's jar is never picked.
